---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import { apps, checkedMonth, features, legend } from "../../content/comparison";

const title = "Pikos vs Notion, Obsidian, NotePlan and others";
const description = "How Pikos compares to Notion, Obsidian, Logseq, NotePlan, TickTick, Things, Todoist, and the Apple apps. Honest feature comparison.";

const columns = features.filter((f) => f.onSite);
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-8 py-20">

    <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-neutral-400 transition-colors hover:text-primary-500 dark:text-neutral-500 dark:hover:text-primary-400">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m15 18-6-6 6-6"/></svg>
      Home
    </a>

    <h1 class="mt-8 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">How Pikos compares</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">
      Most of these apps are built around one thing and add the other two. TickTick and Todoist are task managers whose calendar views cost extra. Obsidian is a notes app whose tasks and calendar come from plugins. In Pikos all three are the same page: write it, tick it off, put it on a day. It's desktop only for now, with no phone app and no sync yet.
    </p>

    <!-- Comparison table -->
    <div class="mt-12 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-700">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[36rem] text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:bg-neutral-900">
            <th class="w-[100px] px-3 py-2.5 text-left text-xs font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">App</th>
            {columns.map((col) => (
              <th class="px-2 py-2.5 text-center text-xs font-semibold text-neutral-900 last:pr-3 dark:text-neutral-50">{col.label}</th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {apps.map((app, i) => (
            <tr class:list={[
              "border-b border-neutral-100 last:border-b-0 dark:border-neutral-800",
              i === 0 && "bg-warning-400/10 dark:bg-warning-500/15",
            ]}>
              <td class:list={[
                "px-3 py-2.5 text-left font-medium",
                i === 0 ? "text-warning-500 dark:text-warning-400" : "text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50",
              ]}>
                {app.name}
              </td>
              {columns.map((col) => {
                const val = app[col.key];
                return (
                  <td class="px-2 py-2.5 text-center last:pr-3">
                    {val === true ? (
                      <svg class="mx-auto h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"/></svg>
                    ) : typeof val === "string" ? (
                      <span class="whitespace-nowrap text-xs text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">{val}</span>
                    ) : (
                      <span class="text-neutral-300 dark:text-neutral-600">—</span>
                    )}
                  </td>
                );
              })}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500">
      {legend(columns)} Checked in {checkedMonth()} against each app's own pricing and help pages. These apps change often, so if something here is out of date, <a href="/feedback" class="hover:text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:hover:text-primary-400">send a correction</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- Prose comparison sections -->
    <div class="mt-16 space-y-10 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-300">

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">What the table can't show</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          In most of these apps a note, a task and an event are three separate things, even when one app holds all three. In Pikos they're one page. Write the notes from a meeting, tick off what came out of it, drag it onto Thursday, and it's still the same page. Notion can do this once you've built a database for it, and NotePlan comes close, since its tasks live inside notes. In Pikos it's how every page works, with nothing to set up.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The same goes for calendar events. Write on one, tag it or tick it off, and it becomes your page. Disconnect the calendar later, or delete the event there, and the page stays in Pikos. Events you never touched are removed.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Which one fits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">They're all good apps. Each one is the right pick for someone.</p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Apple Notes, Reminders and Calendar</strong> if you want free, already installed, and on your phone.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">NotePlan</strong> if you want all three on Mac and iPhone today, as Markdown files, and a subscription is fine.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Notion</strong> if you work with other people, or you want databases.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Obsidian</strong> if your work is writing and linking notes, and you enjoy shaping your tools.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Logseq</strong> if you think in outlines and daily journals, and want open source.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">TickTick</strong> if you're tasks first, on every platform, with habits.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Things</strong> if you want a focused, beautifully made task manager on Apple devices.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Todoist</strong> if you want tasks everywhere, shared with other people.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-medium text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos</strong> if you want your notes, tasks and calendar to be one thing, on your computer, with no account and no subscription.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Apple Notes, Reminders, and Calendar</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          You already have all three. They're free and they sync to your phone with no setup, which Pikos can't match yet. Reminders now show up in Calendar, but notes still sit apart: the note from a meeting and the task it produced live in different apps. In Pikos they're one page, sitting on the day it happened.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <a href="/compare/apple" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">The longer comparison</a>, including what to do about years of existing Apple Notes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Obsidian</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Obsidian is the better tool for linking notes into a knowledge base, and it runs everywhere, phones included. Pikos doesn't link pages yet. Out of the box Obsidian has checkboxes but no due dates and no calendar, so plugins fill the gap. Pikos has both built in.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <a href="/compare/obsidian" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">The longer comparison</a>, including moving a vault across.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Notion</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Notion does all three and reaches much further: shared workspaces, databases, a phone app. It keeps your work on its servers, needs an account, and works offline only for pages you've marked. Pikos keeps everything on your computer and needs no account. If you work with other people, Notion is the better tool.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <a href="/compare/notion" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">The longer comparison</a>, including what an export does and doesn't bring.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs NotePlan</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The closest match. Notes, tasks and a calendar, stored as Markdown files on your device, with iPhone and iPad apps and iCloud sync that Pikos doesn't have yet. It's $99 a year after a short trial. Pikos is a free download, and its App Store and iPhone versions will be one-time purchases.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs TickTick</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The closest to Pikos. It's a task manager first. Its list view has a proper editor beside each task, but from the calendar you get a small popover, and a note is a separate kind of item you convert a task into. The calendar views need Premium. TickTick runs everywhere and syncs, which Pikos doesn't yet, but it needs an account and keeps your data on its servers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Things</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          A beautifully made task manager that keeps your data on your devices. To-dos can hold notes and your calendar events show up in Today, but there are no standalone notes and no calendar view. It's $49.99 once on Mac, with separate iPhone and iPad apps. If a focused task manager is all you want, Things is hard to beat.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">You can always take it with you</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Pikos imports Markdown folders and TickTick or Todoist exports. Everything you write is one file on your computer, so backing up is copying it. Leaving is just as easy: Pikos exports Markdown, CSV and calendar files other apps can open.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>

  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>

  </style>
